# Copy to `.env` and edit. `docker compose` reads it automatically.
# No secrets belong in this file — Tesserafin's admin account is created in the
# browser on first run, not here.

# Path to your media library on the host. Mounted READ-ONLY at /media.
# Examples:
#   Linux/NAS : TESSERAFIN_MEDIA=/mnt/user/media
#   macOS     : TESSERAFIN_MEDIA=/Users/you/Media
# Leave unset to start with an empty ./media folder and add real media later.
#TESSERAFIN_MEDIA=/path/to/your/media

# Pin or override the image. docker-compose.yml defaults to an IMMUTABLE
# reference on purpose — never `latest`, `preview`, or a moving major/minor tag —
# so `docker compose pull` cannot change your version, or run a schema migration,
# at a moment you did not choose.
#
# Strongest form, a digest pin (survives a tag being re-pointed):
#TESSERAFIN_IMAGE=ghcr.io/tesserafin-project/tesserafin@sha256:29493c0ecf956a61f06c2b801e7c867d560fb95ab184c09d05fdb6db508a7722
# Or an exact immutable version tag:
#TESSERAFIN_IMAGE=ghcr.io/tesserafin-project/tesserafin:12.0.0-dev.a8a18bb7c07b
#
# Moving channels (`latest`, `preview`, `1`, `1.0`) are an explicit opt-in, not
# a default — and none of them is published yet. Read the risks and the upgrade
# procedure first: docs/container/A6-versioning-and-upgrades.md (#92 / [A6]).
#
# TRANSITIONAL: the two references above are pre-v1 development artifacts in the
# frozen archive `ghcr.io/tesserafin-project/tesserafin`. The canonical v1+
# package is `ghcr.io/tesserafin-project/tesserafin-server`; these examples move
# to it once the first 1.0.0 development baseline is published.
# See docs/versioning-policy.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability (OPTIONAL — issue #91 / [A5])
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The container already reports its own health: `docker compose ps` shows
# (healthy) once GET http://<host>:8096/health answers 200. That endpoint runs a
# real query against the database, so it goes unhealthy on a broken database and
# not merely when the process has died. It needs no credentials and returns:
#
#   {"status":"healthy","version":"12.0.0","database":"healthy"}
#
# Minimum log level. Valid values: Verbose, Debug, Information, Warning, Error,
# Fatal. Anything else is ignored with a warning — the server still starts.
# Change it and re-run `docker compose up -d`; no rebuild is needed.
#TESSERAFIN_LOG_LEVEL=Information

# Console log rendering: `json` (the container default — one JSON object per
# line, which is what log collectors want) or `text` for human reading.
#TESSERAFIN_LOG_FORMAT=json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardware acceleration (OPTIONAL — Linux + VAAPI only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# You do not need anything below to run Tesserafin. By default the server probes
# for hardware at every start and transcodes in software when it finds none.
# These two settings apply only when you add the opt-in override:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.vaapi.yml up -d
#
# See docs/container/A4-hardware-acceleration.md for the full walkthrough.

# The GPU render node to hand to the container. Check what you have with:
#   ls -l /dev/dri/renderD*
#TESSERAFIN_RENDER_DEVICE=/dev/dri/renderD128

# The NUMERIC group id owning that render node on the HOST. Find it with:
#   stat -c '%g %G' /dev/dri/renderD128
# It is usually the `render` group, but the number varies by distribution — do
# not copy someone else's. A wrong value is safe: the container cannot open the
# device, the startup probe fails, and the server transcodes in software.
#TESSERAFIN_RENDER_GID=993
